{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ומחוקק מבין רגליו", :translit "u'mechokek mibein raglav", :gloss "and the lawgiver from between his feet"} {:he "תלמידי חכמים", :translit "talmidei chachamim", :gloss "disciples of the wise"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "ומחוקק מבין רגליו", :b "תלמידי חכמים"}], :frame {:text "Genesis 49:10 records Jacob's blessing to Judah: 'the scepter shall not depart from Judah, nor the lawgiver from between his feet, until Shiloh come.' The phrase 'and the lawgiver from between his feet' (ומחוקק מבין רגליו, Gen 49:10) is read as referring to scholars who transmit Torah law.", :sources ["Genesis 49:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מחקק", :translit "mechokek", :gloss "lawgiver"} {:he "ערוכה בכל ושמורה", :translit "aruchah b'chol u'shemurah", :gloss "ordered in all things and kept"} {:he "יבא שילה", :translit "yavo shiloh", :gloss "Shiloh shall come"} {:he "משיח", :translit "mashiach", :gloss "anointed one"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "מחקק", :b "רמ״ח"} {:kind :other, :note "248 limbs of the human body correspond to 248 positive commandments"} {:kind :gematria, :a "יבא שילה", :b "משיח"}], :frame {:text "The Mishnah teaches that a person has 248 limbs corresponding to the 248 positive commandments (Makkot 23b). The verse describing Torah as 'ordered in all things and kept' (II Samuel 23:5) is linked to the 248-word structure of the Shema (Berakhot 14b). Genesis 49:10's phrase 'until Shiloh come' (יבא שילה) is read messianically in rabbinic tradition; Targum Onkelos renders it 'until the Messiah comes, whose is the kingship.'", :sources ["Makkot 23b" "Berakhot 14b" "II Samuel 23:5" "Genesis 49:10" "Targum Onkelos on Genesis 49:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "שילה", :translit "shiloh", :gloss "Shiloh"} {:he "משה", :translit "mosheh", :gloss "Moses"} {:he "ויתא ראשי עם", :translit "vayita rashei am", :gloss "and he pitched the heads of the people"} {:he "שרי יהודה רגמתם", :translit "sarei yehudah ragmatam", :gloss "the leaders of Judah rallied them"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "שילה", :b "משה"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'and he pitched the heads of the people' (ויתא ראשי עם) appears in Deuteronomy 33:21 as part of Moses' blessing, describing one who provides leadership for the people. The parallel phrase 'Judah's leaders rallied them' (שרי יהודה רגמתם) appears in Judges 20:11, describing the tribe of Judah's mobilization. The Ba'al HaTurim links these two verses to connect Moses and Judah, suggesting that at the moment Moses gathered the people's leaders, Judah was set apart for sanctification.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 33:21" "Judges 20:11" "Genesis 49:10"]}}], :arithmetic [{:a-value 611, :b-value 612, :lens :standard, :equal? false, :kind :gematria, :b "תלמידי חכמים", :by :selah, :colel? true, :a "ומחוקק מבין רגליו"} {:kind :gematria, :a "מחקק", :a-value 248, :b "רמ״ח", :b-value 248, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :other, :note "248 limbs of the human body correspond to 248 positive commandments", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "יבא שילה", :a-value 358, :b "משיח", :b-value 358, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :gematria, :a "שילה", :a-value 345, :b "משה", :b-value 345, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:12:28.187571800Z", :model "glm-5.2"}